"""Original political-economy and distributional reasoning exercises."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(75, 'Social Policy Political Economy and Inequality', 'Social Policy\nPolitical Economy\nand Inequality',
        'Analyse a fictional policy choice through distribution, institutional incentives and evidence limits, distinguishing explanation from unsupported allegations.', [
        m('Examine distribution and effective benefit', 'Distinguish equal nominal provision from equitable access and adequate support.', [
            'A distributional question asks who receives a policy benefit, who bears a cost and who remains outside its practical reach. Equal nominal payments do not necessarily produce equal usable support when access costs, responsibilities or available services differ. The ILO distinguishes coverage and adequacy, helping a policy reader avoid treating a total expenditure figure as proof of sufficient protection. Begin with the policy purpose and the supplied fictional circumstances. Do not infer the needs or conduct of a real household from a category label or assume that a payment determines every aspect of wellbeing. [S51]',
            'Separate the allocation rule from the resulting pattern. A universal rule, a categorical rule and a means-related rule pose different questions about inclusion, administration and financing, but their labels do not establish how an actual system performs. Read the invented eligibility description and compare it with evidence of receipt, regularity and remaining need. Administrative records can omit people who cannot reach an enrolment process. A count of recipients is therefore not automatically a count of everyone entitled to support, and absence from a record does not by itself justify exclusion. [S51]',
            'A useful distribution note states its denominator and period, identifies which costs the supplied figures include and avoids hiding differences behind an average. If two fictional routes receive the same travel amount but one has a higher journey cost, compare the remaining barrier rather than simply declaring the scheme equal. Do not invent a monetary threshold or a real legal entitlement. Describe what information an authorised policy team would need to judge adequacy and what the case cannot tell us about individual circumstances or longer-term effects. [S51]',
            'Research can challenge a simple assumption that a financial input guarantees all desired outcomes. The US randomised cash-transfer study reports particular outcomes in a specific setting, with measurement and transfer limits. Its mixed findings neither prove that money never matters nor guarantee that any local policy will work identically. State the relevant comparison and outcome before using it to inform a fictional option. The purpose of appraisal is to sharpen a distributional question, not to replace a reasoned policy choice with a universal slogan. [R61]'],
            'Two fictional service routes receive the same travel payment. The supplied case gives different journey costs and appointment availability, while the draft calls equal payments proof of equal access.',
            'Write a distribution note separating the nominal payment, practical cost, service availability and remaining uncertainty. Identify the denominator you would need for a coverage statement.',
            'Equal amounts do not establish equal usable support. Access conditions and actual receipt must be examined alongside a clear population and period.', ['S51', 'R61'], [
                ('Nominal equality differs from usable support.', 'Equal fictional travel payments meet different journey costs. What remains to assess?', 'The remaining practical access barrier on each route.', ['Equality of payment proves equality of access.', 'Every household must have identical needs.', 'The payment creates all missing appointments.'], 'An equal input can have different practical value under different conditions.'),
                ('Coverage statements need a defined population.', 'What is missing from a claim that 200 fictional payments mean complete coverage?', 'A relevant denominator and evidence about intended users not paid.', ['A larger publicity font.', 'Names of every real claimant.', 'An assumption that all residents are ineligible.'], 'A payment count alone does not establish complete reach.'),
                ('Administrative absence is not automatic ineligibility.', 'A person is absent from an invented payment list. What can be concluded?', 'The list alone does not establish entitlement or eligibility.', ['They are certainly ineligible.', 'They must have rejected all support.', 'Every administrative record is false.'], 'Access barriers and recording limits can affect who appears in a list.'),
                ('Policy labels do not settle performance.', 'A fictional scheme is called universal. What still needs evidence?', 'Actual receipt, adequacy and delivery conditions.', ['That all intended users automatically received it.', 'That all other designs are inferior.', 'That no administration is required.'], 'The stated rule and its implementation are different questions.'),
                ('Outcome-specific research resists universal slogans.', 'Which interpretation of the US transfer trial is defensible?', 'Its outcome-specific findings need context before informing a local option.', ['Money never affects any outcome.', 'Every cash scheme guarantees the same gains.', 'The journal rank settles policy design.'], 'A particular comparison cannot support either universal slogan.')]),
        m('Analyse incentives power and institutional response', 'Map a plausible institutional mechanism without alleging motives from incomplete evidence.', [
            'Political-economy analysis considers how resources, interests, institutions and power shape policy choices and implementation. It should explain a plausible mechanism rather than label an individual virtuous or corrupt without evidence. In the fictional case, distinguish the person who benefits from a decision, the role that has authority and the institution that must answer questions. An actor can support a reform for several reasons, and a delayed response does not reveal a private motive. Write hypotheses that could be checked against appropriate records rather than treating a suspicion as a demonstrated cause. [R64]',
            'The Nigerian digital-budget case uses an abductive approach to develop propositions about accessible information and public value. This makes it useful for questioning how disclosure connects to capacity, incentives and response arrangements. It does not measure a universal effect of budget portals or establish a binding reform rule. Ask whether the invented office has a reason and ability to respond, whether another role can scrutinise its explanation and whether records allow follow-up. Keep the study\'s contextual limitations visible when moving from a proposed mechanism to an educational recommendation. [R64]',
            'Power can affect whose concerns receive attention and whose evidence becomes visible. For a supplied fictional decision, identify the groups represented in the record, the perspectives missing and the practical conditions that may constrain participation. OECD guidance asks for an honest purpose, appropriate methods and a connection between input and decisions. A stakeholder map should therefore include the limits of each role\'s influence and a way to explain the eventual choice. It must not become a list of real private identities or a promise that every preference can be satisfied. [S53]',
            'Turn the analysis into a bounded reform question. If a fictional portal publishes figures but no role answers discrepancies, a plausible improvement concerns response ownership and follow-up, not simply another upload. State what would need authorisation, which resource or incentive may support the change and what observation would challenge your interpretation. Avoid assuming that all resistance is misconduct or that public visibility alone removes institutional constraints. A credible political-economy note can describe competing explanations and a practical test while leaving motives and causal effects unproven. [R64]'],
            'A fictional portal team is rewarded for uploads, while discrepancy responses are assigned to nobody. A draft accuses staff of deliberately concealing all errors without further evidence.',
            'Map the target, authority and missing response responsibility. Offer two explanations for the gap and a bounded change with a testable observation.',
            'The upload incentive and unassigned response are evidenced process conditions. Intentional concealment is not established; clarify ownership and monitor a defined response process.', ['R64', 'S53'], [
                ('Institutional explanation differs from an unsupported motive claim.', 'What is evidenced by an invented upload target with no response owner?', 'An incentive and responsibility gap that may affect responses.', ['Deliberate concealment by every staff member.', 'Proof that all information is false.', 'A universal law of corruption.'], 'The records support a process hypothesis, not a finding about private motives.'),
                ('Mechanisms should be testable.', 'Which question tests the fictional response mechanism?', 'Does assigning an authorised response role produce documented explanations?', ['Are all opponents morally bad?', 'Can publicity replace every operating resource?', 'Does one upload prove every concern resolved?'], 'The question connects a proposed institutional change to observable process evidence.'),
                ('Actor influence and decision power differ.', 'Why distinguish a consulted group from the fictional decision owner?', 'Their ability to contribute and authority to decide are not identical.', ['Consultation always transfers every legal power.', 'Only decision owners can have relevant knowledge.', 'All preferences must be adopted.'], 'An influence map should represent actual roles without promising powers.'),
                ('Case propositions retain contextual limits.', 'What would overstate the Nigerian digital-budget study?', 'Claiming it measured a guaranteed corruption reduction from every portal.', ['Using it to question response capacity.', 'Recording its abductive design.', 'Checking differences in the fictional setting.'], 'Contextual propositions are not universal causal effect estimates.'),
                ('Competing explanations should remain examinable.', 'What strengthens a fictional political-economy note?', 'Evidence, alternative explanations and an observation that could change the view.', ['A single accusation with no records.', 'Deletion of contrary evidence.', 'A promise that one reform ends every conflict.'], 'Explicit alternatives make the mechanism open to scrutiny.')]),
        m('Compare policy options and communicate trade-offs', 'Make an evidence-bounded recommendation that explains distribution and uncertainty.', [
            'Comparing social-policy options requires a common question, a defined purpose and an honest account of trade-offs. An option may improve one access barrier while increasing an administrative burden or depending on a service that is not available. Describe intended benefits, likely costs, delivery requirements and the groups whose circumstances remain uncertain. The ILO distinction between coverage and adequacy helps prevent a simple ranking based only on recipient totals. The classroom comparison should show why a stated criterion matters rather than manufacture a single score that hides incompatible objectives. [S51]',
            'Evidence from different designs cannot be ranked as if every study asked the same question. The Brazilian cash-transfer cohort concerns a selected population and historical administrative records, with adjustment and remaining confounding. It does not directly randomise conditional against unconditional transfers. The US study randomises different payment amounts in another population and reports outcome-specific findings. Putting their headlines side by side cannot establish which policy design is globally superior. First describe each comparison, outcome and limitation; then explain the narrow way it can inform the fictional choice. [R62] [R61]',
            'Use a transparent options note with the same headings for each proposal: intended mechanism, distribution, resources, delivery dependencies, evidence and uncertainty. If the supplied case cannot establish a cost or effect, label it unknown and specify a responsible next check. Do not fill a gap with an invented empirical result or imply that a classroom arithmetic example is a published estimate. Digital-budget inquiry also reminds us that accessible information needs an institutional response, so an option relying on disclosure should include who can examine and act on the resulting questions. [R64]',
            'A recommendation can be conditional. Explain which option appears more suitable under the stated fictional assumptions, what would need verification before implementation and what finding would change the judgement. Record any distributional concern that remains unresolved instead of concealing it to strengthen the recommendation. Public decisions involve authority and values as well as evidence; an educational analysis can clarify those choices without authorising expenditure or deciding real entitlements. A useful conclusion is specific enough to review and modest enough to remain faithful to the available information. [S51]'],
            'A fictional team ranks two support options using a US trial headline and a Brazilian cohort headline. Its preferred option has unknown staffing costs and no plan for the people unable to enrol online.',
            'Replace the headline ranking with a two-option note. Identify the unsupported comparison, an unresolved distribution issue and a condition for any recommendation.',
            'The studies address different comparisons and populations. Keep unknown costs and enrolment barriers visible; a conditional recommendation needs an authorised verification step.', ['S51', 'R62', 'R61', 'R64'], [
                ('Options need a common purpose and transparent criteria.', 'What makes the fictional options comparison reviewable?', 'The same explicit purpose and evidence headings for both options.', ['Different success definitions chosen to favour one option.', 'A headline ranking with no methods.', 'A score hiding all missing costs.'], 'Comparable headings expose trade-offs and evidence gaps.'),
                ('Unlike studies do not create a head-to-head comparison.', 'Why can the US trial and Brazilian cohort not settle a universal conditional-versus-unconditional ranking?', 'They study different comparisons, populations and designs.', ['All research is useless for policy.', 'The newer publication automatically wins.', 'The larger journal title establishes superiority.'], 'No direct comparable randomised contrast is created by juxtaposing headlines.'),
                ('Unknown costs should remain explicit.', 'The invented staffing cost is absent. What should the note do?', 'Mark it unknown and identify an appropriate verification task.', ['Invent a precise cost and call it researched.', 'Treat the cost as zero.', 'Delete the resource section.'], 'Missing information is a decision limit, not permission to fabricate.'),
                ('Distributional concerns belong in the recommendation.', 'An online-only option excludes an invented access route. What should the conclusion include?', 'The unresolved barrier and a feasible check or alternative.', ['Only the favourable aggregate count.', 'A claim of universal inclusion.', 'Real personal histories collected by students.'], 'A recommendation should explain who may remain outside practical reach.'),
                ('A conditional recommendation names what could change it.', 'Which conclusion is strongest for incomplete fictional evidence?', 'Prefer an option under stated assumptions, pending specified authorised checks.', ['Implement nationally without further authority.', 'Claim certainty because a Q1 paper was cited.', 'Hide all assumptions to sound decisive.'], 'A bounded recommendation is explicit about evidence and decision conditions.')])])
